title: '2026-11-15: the answer to Jeff''s ''to Mario = Tracy'' is written, under my fork of it, ready for Libby.'
pid: 75d9b658-c29d-457c-9d51-d3a4c47161a4
body: |2-
  The root is [[id:71fea003-732f-4c14-ac71-b98bc05de105][the answer]], a child of the fork
  87696c7a-1b9b-405c-b8ce-0cb246b867af. Its recommendation: a rented
  4-vCPU, 8 GB Linux machine is enough, at about €30 a month; missions
  wait on the model, not on the tests.
  Not measured: nothing ran on a rented machine (four of this laptop's
  efficiency cores stood in, and a shared rented vCPU may be slower);
  ARM (EC2 t4g, Oracle) is untested; Contabo's speed is unknown; EC2's
  outbound traffic and its regional disk price were not confirmed today,
  and Oracle's figures come from news, since its site refused the reader.
  Built: two scripts in 'tools/introspect/machine-measures/',
  'measure_run.py' and 'where_sessions_wait.py', not committed; they are
  code, for you to commit or drop. What they do is in Tracy's graph.
  Side effects: I touched 'server/serve.rs' (its time only) three times
  to force rebuilds, so the next build recompiles it; one run of
  'bash/all-tests.sh' replaced 'tests/results/' at 01:26 UTC (all
  passed). The scratch builds are deleted.
  What surprised me: the whole suite uses under one core on average; the
  model's own time was 77% of all working time over five days; and
  Jeff's server has 277 MB swapped out.
  Better suited to another: the time each session spends per kind of
  tool call could be a column of the spending account, which would make
  the premise check here a query instead of a script.
contains:
- 5efc7b91-fae9-4748-8218-43f008a3cf7a
